##### MF1: GENERAL INFORMATION                                    1025 1451  170
                                                                  1025 1451  171
- MT451    : Descriptive data and directory                       1025 1451  172
                                                                  1025 1451  173
This text and the full directory of used MF/MT sections.          1025 1451  174
                                                                  1025 1451  175
##### MF2: RESONANCE PARAMETERS                                   1025 1451  176
                                                                  1025 1451  177
- MT151    : Resonance parameters                                 1025 1451  178
                                                                  1025 1451  179
Details about the origin of the resonance parameters for this     1025 1451  180
nuclide are given above in this description. In general, both a   1025 1451  181
Resolved and an Unresolved Resonance Range are given.             1025 1451  182
                                                                  1025 1451  183
##### MF3: REACTION CROSS SECTIONS                                1025 1451  184
                                                                  1025 1451  185
All the data present in the following MT-sections have been       1025 1451  186
calculated with TALYS, possibly normalized to experimental data.  1025 1451  187
If the maximal cross section in an excitation function over the   1025 1451  188
whole energy range does not exceed 1.e-9 b, the MT-number is not  1025 1451  189
included. Cross sections lower than 1.e-20 b are assumed to have  1025 1451  190
no physical meaning and are set to zero. All MT numbers extend up 1025 1451  191
to the highest incident energy.                                   1025 1451  192
The following reaction channels/MT numbers are generally included:1025 1451  193
                                                                  1025 1451  194
- MT1      : Total cross section                                  1025 1451  195
                                                                  1025 1451  196
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range,    1025 1451  197
or partly reproduced using experimental data.                     1025 1451  198
                                                                  1025 1451  199
- MT2      : Elastic scattering cross section                     1025 1451  200
                                                                  1025 1451  201
Obtained by subtracting the non-elastic cross section from the    1025 1451  202
total cross section.                                              1025 1451  203
                                                                  1025 1451  204
- MT3      : Non-elastic cross section                            1025 1451  205
                                                                  1025 1451  206
Calculated with the optical model over the whole energy range.    1025 1451  207
                                                                  1025 1451  208
- MT4      : Total inelastic cross section                        1025 1451  209
                                                                  1025 1451  210
Sum of MT=51-91.                                                  1025 1451  211
                                                                  1025 1451  212
- MT5      : (n,anything) cross section                           1025 1451  213
                                                                  1025 1451  214
MT5 contains the reactions which can not be stored in any other   1025 1451  215
MT-number. The information of MF3/MT5 can be combined with        1025 1451  216
MF6/MT5 to obtain residual production cross sections particle     1025 1451  217
production cross sections and (double-)differential cross         1025 1451  218
sections.                                                         1025 1451  219
                                                                  1025 1451  220
- MT16     : (n,2n) cross section                                 1025 1451  221
- MT17     : (n,3n) cross section                                 1025 1451  222
- MT22     : (n,na) cross section                                 1025 1451  223
- MT28     : (n,np) cross section                                 1025 1451  224
- MT51-90  : (n,n') cross section for 1st-40th excited state      1025 1451  225
- MT91     : (n,n') continuum cross section                       1025 1451  226
- MT102    : (n,gamma) cross section                              1025 1451  227
- MT103    : (n,p) cross section                                  1025 1451  228
- MT104    : (n,d) cross section                                  1025 1451  229
- MT105    : (n,t) cross section                                  1025 1451  230
- MT106    : (n,h) cross section                                  1025 1451  231
- MT107    : (n,a) cross section                                  1025 1451  232
- MT600-610: (n,p) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1025 1451  233
- MT649    : (n,p) continuum cross section                        1025 1451  234
- MT650-655: (n,d) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1025 1451  235
- MT699    : (n,d) continuum cross section                        1025 1451  236
- MT700-705: (n,t) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1025 1451  237
- MT749    : (n,t) continuum cross section                        1025 1451  238
- MT750-755: (n,h) cross section for 0th-5th excited state        1025 1451  239
- MT799    : (n,h) continuum cross section                        1025 1451  240
- MT800-810: (n,a) cross section for 0th-10th excited state       1025 1451  241
- MT849    : (n,a) continuum cross section                        1025 1451  242
                                                                  1025 1451  243
##### MF4: ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS OF SECONDARY PARTICLES           1025 1451  244
                                                                  1025 1451  245
We use MF4 for the neutron scattering angular distribution for    1025 1451  246
discrete levels.                                                  1025 1451  247
                                                                  1025 1451  248
- MT2      : Elastic angular distribution                         1025 1451  249
                                                                  1025 1451  250
The flag LTT=3 is used to indicate a switch at some high energy,  1025 1451  251
generally 50 MeV, from a Legendre representation to a tabulated   1025 1451  252
representation. For incident energies below this energy, the      1025 1451  253
Legendre coefficients are given on a sufficiently precise energy  1025 1451  254
grid. They are a sum of calculated Legendre coefficients for      1025 1451  255
compound nucleus and shape-elastic scattering. For higher         1025 1451  256
incident energies, relative angular distributions are tabulated   1025 1451  257
on an angular grid.                                               1025 1451  258
                                                                  1025 1451  259
- MT51-70  : (n,n') angular distribution for 1st-20th state       1025 1451  260
- MT600-610: (n,p) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1025 1451  261
- MT650-655: (n,d) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1025 1451  262
- MT700-705: (n,t) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1025 1451  263
- MT750-755: (n,h) angular distribution for 0th-5th state         1025 1451  264
- MT800-810: (n,a) angular distribution for 0th-10th state        1025 1451  265
                                                                  1025 1451  266
##### MF6: PRODUCT ENERGY-ANGLE DISTRIBUTIONS                     1025 1451  267
                                                                  1025 1451  268
In MF6 we store all secondary energy, angle, and energy-angle     1025 1451  269
distributions, as well as all residual and discrete + continuum   1025 1451  270
photon production cross sections for channels other than capture  1025 1451  271
(MT102) and reactions to discrete levels (MT51,etc.).             1025 1451  272
All data are generated with TALYS.                                1025 1451  273
                                                                  1025 1451  274
- MT5      : (n,anything) yields and energy-angle distributions   1025 1451  275
                                                                  1025 1451  276
MT5 contains, in this order,                                      1025 1451  277
For neutrons up to alpha particles and photons:                   1025 1451  278
- the production yields per particle for the whole energy range   1025 1451  279
- the secondary energy-angle distributions per particles which are1025 1451  280
  not represented by explicit MT numbers. On a secondary energy   1025 1451  281
  grid the relative emission spectra are given together with the  1025 1451  282
  parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular              1025 1451  283
  distributions. Inelastic scattering cross sections for discrete 1025 1451  284
  states have been broadened and added to the continuum spectra.  1025 1451  285
For each residual product:                                        1025 1451  286
- the production yields for the whole energy range.               1025 1451  287
- the recoil                                                      1025 1451  288
These distributions can be multiplied with MF3/MT5 to get         1025 1451  289
the production cross sections and (double-)differential cross     1025 1451  290
sections.                                                         1025 1451  291
                                                                  1025 1451  292
- MT16     : (n,2n) energy-angle distr. and photon production     1025 1451  293
                                                                  1025 1451  294
First, for each type of outgoing particle, the (trivial) integer  1025 1451  295
particle yields are given. Next, on a sufficiently dense incident 1025 1451  296
energy grid the secondary energy-angle distributions are          1025 1451  297
specified by means of the relative emission spectra and the       1025 1451  298
parameters for the Kalbach systematics for angular distributions. 1025 1451  299
Next, the photon yield is tabulated as a function of incident     1025 1451  300
energy. For each incident energy, the photon production is given  1025 1451  301
for all discrete gamma lines present in the final nucleus. A      1025 1451  302
continuum photon distribution is added to this. We assume         1025 1451  303
isotropy for all produced gamma rays.                             1025 1451  304
                                                                  1025 1451  305
For the following MT-numbers, the same procedure as for MT16 is   1025 1451  306
followed:                                                         1025 1451  307
                                                                  1025 1451  308
- MT17 : (n,3n) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1025 1451  309
- MT22 : (n,na) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1025 1451  310
- MT28 : (n,np) energy-angle distr. and photon production         1025 1451  311
- MT91 : (n,n') continuum energy-angle distr. and phot. prod.     1025 1451  312
- MT649: (n,p) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1025 1451  313
- MT699: (n,d) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1025 1451  314
- MT749: (n,t) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1025 1451  315
- MT809: (n,h) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1025 1451  316
- MT849: (n,a) continuum energy-angle distr. and photon prod.     1025 1451  317
                                                                  1025 1451  318
##### MF8: RADIOACTIVE DECAY DATA                                 1025 1451  319
                                                                  1025 1451  320
For reactions to isomers, MF8 designates where the information    1025 1451  321
for isomeric versus ground state production, or residual          1025 1451  322
production can be found, i.e. for each MT number it points to     1025 1451  323
either MF6, MF9 or MF10.                                          1025 1451  324
                                                                  1025 1451  325
##### MF9: MULTIPLICITIES FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES  1025 1451  326
                                                                  1025 1451  327
MF9 is used for isomer production by non-threshold reactions.     1025 1451  328
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1025 1451  329
as isomer.                                                        1025 1451  330
                                                                  1025 1451  331
##### MF10: CROSS SECTIONS FOR PRODUCTION OF RADIOACTIVE NUCLIDES 1025 1451  332
                                                                  1025 1451  333
MF10 is used for isomer production by threshold reactions.        1025 1451  334
Final states with a lifetime that exceeds 1 second are treated    1025 1451  335
as isomer.                                                        1025 1451  336
                                                                  1025 1451  337
##### MF12: PHOTON PRODUCTION YIELD DATA                          1025 1451  338
                                                                  1025 1451  339
MF12 is used for the photon production for two types of reactions:1025 1451  340
- Reactions to discrete levels (MT51, etc.) For this transition   1025 1451  341
  probabilities are used (LO=2)                                   1025 1451  342
- Photons from neutron capture (MT102). Here multiplicities are   1025 1451  343
  given                                                           1025 1451  344
                                                                  1025 1451  345
##### MF14: PHOTON ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                          1025 1451  346
                                                                  1025 1451  347
All photon production is considered as isotropic                  1025 1451  348
                                                                  1025 1451  349
##### MF15: PHOTON ENERGY SPECTRA                                 1025 1451  350
                                                                  1025 1451  351
For capture (MT102), the photon energy spectrum is given.         1025 1451  352
                                                                  1025 1451  353
##### MF32: COVARIANCES OF RESONANCE PARAMETERS                   1025 1451  354
                                                                  1025 1451  355
In MF32, we store the covariances of resonance parameters,        1025 1451  356
and more details are given above in this description. In most     1025 1451  357
versions of TENDL, this information is now stored in MF33.        1025 1451  358
                                                                  1025 1451  359
##### MF33: COVARIANCES OF NEUTRON CROSS SECTIONS                 1025 1451  360
                                                                  1025 1451  361
In MF33, we store the covariances of most, but not all, reaction  1025 1451  362
channels that are given in MF3. All covariances are generated     1025 1451  363
with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates TALYS runs using    1025 1451  364
a Monte Carlo parameter method.                                   1025 1451  365
For each MT-number, the information consists of:                  1025 1451  366
1. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Ea (variances)                1025 1451  367
2. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTa, Eb (correlations for the      1025 1451  368
   same reaction channel between different energies)              1025 1451  369
3. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Ea (correlations between      1025 1451  370
   different reaction channels for the same energy)               1025 1451  371
4. Covariances for MTa, Ea and MTb, Eb (correlations between      1025 1451  372
   different reaction channels for different energies)            1025 1451  373
Classes 1 and 2 are covered by NI-type subsubsections with        1025 1451  374
flag LB=5, followed by a LB=8 section which contains              1025 1451  375
the cross section variances multiplied by 1.e-3. Next, classes 3  1025 1451  376
and 4 are covered by flag LB=6 to account for the different       1025 1451  377
energy grids that two correlating MT-sections may have. Since     1025 1451  378
the total correlation matrix is fully symmetric, we only need to  1025 1451  379
include the correlation elements between different MT-numbers     1025 1451  380
once, and we do that in the subsection with the lowest of the     1025 1451  381
two MT-numbers. We include covariance information for all the     1025 1451  382
total cross sections, the lumped partial cross sections and the   1025 1451  383
individual inelastic cross sections per level (MT51,...). We do   1025 1451  384
not include covariances for MT600 and higher MT-numbers. For the  1025 1451  385
most important channels, we include cross-channel correlations.   1025 1451  386
                                                                  1025 1451  387
##### MF34: COVARIANCES OF ANGULAR DISTRIBUTIONS                  1025 1451  388
                                                                  1025 1451  389
In MF34, we store the covariances of the elastic scattering       1025 1451  390
angular distributions that are given in MF4/MT2. All covariances  1025 1451  391
are generated with the TASMAN code [kon19], which generates       1025 1451  392
TALYS runs using a Monte Carlo parameter method. The tables are   1025 1451  393
restricted to MT2 and contain covariances for the first 6         1025 1451  394
Legendre coefficients. Both intra- and inter-correlations of the  1025 1451  395
various Legendre coefficients are given. As in MF33, the LB5      1025 1451  396
representation is used.                                           1025 1451  397
                                                                  1025 1451  398
##### MF40: COVARIANCES OF ISOMER PRODUCTION                      1025 1451  399
                                                                  1025 1451  400
For covariances of ground state and isomeric cross sections, the  1025 1451  401
same procedures as for MF33 are used.                             1025 1451  402
                                                                  1025 1451  403
